00:19:18.839 --> 00:19:22.681
from it being a hypothetical question

00:19:22.681 --> 00:19:26.940
to what needs to happen to
be able to bring an anituber

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:28.520
to Japan as a job?

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:30.020
- Yeah.
- Right. Right.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.558
- And so that started my whole process.

00:19:32.558 --> 00:19:34.230
When did you hear about it?

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:35.681
- Yeah, I think it was,

00:19:35.681 --> 00:19:37.098
It must've been around March or February.

00:19:37.098 --> 00:19:38.593
I'm not a 100% sure.

00:19:38.593 --> 00:19:40.834
I think I was on call with Meilyne

00:19:40.834 --> 00:19:43.042
and I think Meilyne brought it up to me

00:19:43.042 --> 00:19:44.604
and it was basically the same thing.

00:19:44.604 --> 00:19:46.741
Where it was like the hypothetical-

00:19:46.741 --> 00:19:48.148
- Do you like Japan?
(guys laughing)

00:19:48.148 --> 00:19:49.748
- Yeah, something like that, right?

00:19:49.748 --> 00:19:51.949
And I think I was just like "Oh wait,"

00:19:51.949 --> 00:19:53.735
well maybe I was in Japan at the time.

00:19:53.735 --> 00:19:55.136
It was something, right?

00:19:55.136 --> 00:19:57.307
The thing where it was like not concrete

00:19:57.307 --> 00:19:58.833
And it was like, hey,
think about it, you know?

00:19:58.833 --> 00:20:00.163
Like do you want to do it?

00:20:00.163 --> 00:20:02.403
And then I think two or three months later

00:20:02.403 --> 00:20:04.511
it became a bit more serious of like,

00:20:04.511 --> 00:20:05.735
okay, do you want to do it?

00:20:05.735 --> 00:20:07.033
I think, was that similar thing to you?

00:20:07.033 --> 00:20:08.104
- Yeah, exactly.

00:20:08.104 --> 00:20:09.614
- I think we had time to think about it

00:20:09.614 --> 00:20:11.126
and then seriously they asked us.

00:20:11.126 --> 00:20:13.886
- Yeah and it went from a hypothetical to,

00:20:13.886 --> 00:20:16.296
oh, oh, this is actually happening.

00:20:16.296 --> 00:20:19.463
I'm moving to Japan and this-

00:20:19.463 --> 00:20:21.197
- 'Cause yeah, I don't
think I've spoke to you

00:20:21.197 --> 00:20:23.135
about your thoughts on it.

00:20:23.135 --> 00:20:25.056
Maybe I might have at the
time, but I can't remember.

00:20:25.056 --> 00:20:28.406
But were you like from the
first time you got heard

00:20:28.406 --> 00:20:31.317
or you were given the offer,
were you gonna take it or?

00:20:31.317 --> 00:20:34.810
- Yeah, I think I was always
going to take it I think

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:36.757
because I had wanted to
move to Japan for a while

00:20:36.757 --> 00:20:38.715
even if it was for a little bit,

00:20:38.715 --> 00:20:41.255
hadn't really put much
thought into how long

00:20:41.255 --> 00:20:43.373
I wanted to stay here but I was thinking,

00:20:43.373 --> 00:20:46.295
you only get this chance
once in your life.

00:20:46.295 --> 00:20:48.784
If I hated it, then like great,

00:20:48.784 --> 00:20:50.143
at least at least I've done it, right?

00:20:50.143 --> 00:20:52.042
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:20:52.042 --> 00:20:55.501
- But I was pretty concrete
when I got the offer

00:20:55.501 --> 00:20:56.795
that I was gonna take it.

00:20:56.795 --> 00:20:59.596
What I was more worried about was how

00:20:59.596 --> 00:21:01.289
it was actually going to happen

00:21:01.289 --> 00:21:02.412
or what the process is-

00:21:02.412 --> 00:21:04.043
- Yeah, I think there's
a lot of stuff like that,

00:21:04.043 --> 00:21:05.901
where I guess 'cause we'd only interacted

00:21:05.901 --> 00:21:08.088
with BookWalker through Meilyne,

00:21:08.088 --> 00:21:10.437
I think maybe we were a bit like skeptical

00:21:10.437 --> 00:21:12.012
about what goes on in the back end, right?

00:21:12.012 --> 00:21:13.931
We were like I don't know
what's happening, right?

00:21:13.931 --> 00:21:15.476
- Yeah.
- But they're great

00:21:15.476 --> 00:21:17.560
by the way, they took
care of us very nicely.

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:19.419
And Meilyne will kill
me if I say otherwise.

00:21:19.419 --> 00:21:20.621
(guys laughing)

00:21:20.621 --> 00:21:24.380
- It's against the contract
to say anything negative.

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:27.869
But I think the huge
advantage for at least you two

00:21:27.869 --> 00:21:29.842
was the fact that and
I know a lot of people

00:21:29.842 --> 00:21:32.142
who don't do this and definitely should

00:21:32.142 --> 00:21:36.278
is that you guys visited
Japan enough times to-

00:21:36.278 --> 00:21:37.817
- Yeah.
- Yeah, twice.

00:21:37.817 --> 00:21:38.650
(laughing)

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:39.483
- Well, I mean, you know.

00:21:39.483 --> 00:21:40.575
- I was there for a while-

00:21:40.575 --> 00:21:42.926
- Yeah but I know a lot of stories

00:21:42.926 --> 00:21:44.766
of like people who move here

00:21:44.766 --> 00:21:46.104
just thinking like, oh,

00:21:46.104 --> 00:21:47.927
Japan is this wonderful place

00:21:47.927 --> 00:21:49.656
and you know, like nothing could go wrong

00:21:49.656 --> 00:21:50.489
and everyone's so friendly.

00:21:50.489 --> 00:21:51.938
And then they move here

00:21:51.938 --> 00:21:55.862
and their expectations are
just completely rejected.

00:21:55.862 --> 00:21:57.835
- Yeah. There's a lot of
stuff that's different

00:21:57.835 --> 00:22:00.337
between visiting and moving here,

00:22:00.337 --> 00:22:01.707
which we can get into later.

00:22:01.707 --> 00:22:03.065
- Yeah, because for me,

00:22:03.065 --> 00:22:06.315
I think it goes back to the mentality of

00:22:06.315 --> 00:22:08.081
I never felt like moving here

00:22:08.081 --> 00:22:11.355
or I never thought I
would want to move here

00:22:11.355 --> 00:22:13.633
because of the fact that I liked anime.

00:22:13.633 --> 00:22:14.844
I think that's the biggest thing.

00:22:14.844 --> 00:22:17.469
If you want to move to
Japan just purely for anime,

00:22:17.469 --> 00:22:18.519
you're gonna have a bad time.

00:22:18.519 --> 00:22:20.549
- Throw away the weeaboo dreams, guys.

00:22:20.549 --> 00:22:21.716
It's not real.

00:22:22.792 --> 00:22:24.535
- There's pretty worse reasons,

00:22:24.535 --> 00:22:26.920
but it's pretty one of the worser ones.

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:31.190
- Yeah, like for me, in terms
of my exposure to anime,

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:34.005
it hasn't actually increased too much

00:22:34.005 --> 00:22:36.042
compared to when I was living in Thailand

00:22:36.042 --> 00:22:39.325
because there is like a massive
anime fandom in Thailand

00:22:39.325 --> 00:22:41.408
and just Asia in general.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:47.655
There's a big difference
between say England to Japan,

00:22:47.655 --> 00:22:49.285
but from Thailand to Japan.

00:22:49.285 --> 00:22:51.689
I still have to watch anime online

00:22:51.689 --> 00:22:53.480
because you can't watch on TV

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:54.820
'cause I don't understand it yet.

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.121
- Yeah and even If you did,

00:22:56.121 --> 00:22:57.902
the schedule of airing is so weird.

00:22:57.902 --> 00:22:59.425
- Yeah, I'm not gonna stay up till 3:00 am

00:22:59.425 --> 00:23:01.662
to watch-
- It's so sporadic.

00:23:01.662 --> 00:23:03.828
- It's just not worth it.
- Exactly.

00:23:03.828 --> 00:23:04.873
- It kind of like reminds me of

00:23:04.873 --> 00:23:06.790
like old cartoon
broadcast schedule, right?

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:09.492
Like it's all over the place
and repeats constantly.

00:23:09.492 --> 00:23:10.325
Like it's weird.

00:23:10.325 --> 00:23:13.463
Yeah, anyway, I think when I
was presented with the offer

00:23:13.463 --> 00:23:16.003
I didn't initially want,
I wasn't that keen on it

00:23:16.003 --> 00:23:18.644
mainly because I didn't
really know anything.

00:23:18.644 --> 00:23:19.832
And when someone's like, "Hey,

00:23:19.832 --> 00:23:21.729
do you want to come here to this country?"

00:23:21.729 --> 00:23:23.204
And it's like, okay, I mean, Japan's cool,

00:23:23.204 --> 00:23:24.838
but I don't-
- It's a big decision.

00:23:24.838 --> 00:23:28.204
- Yeah, I literally don't know anything.

00:23:28.204 --> 00:23:30.570
I think over time, I just,
especially when I found out

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:32.209
that you were gonna go 100%,

00:23:32.209 --> 00:23:33.231
I think that was like, all right,

00:23:33.231 --> 00:23:35.435
well at least I have my boys here.

00:23:35.435 --> 00:23:38.476
So if I do get lonely, we good.

00:23:38.476 --> 00:23:41.647
But I mean, yeah, meeting
friends wasn't a problem anyway

00:23:41.647 --> 00:23:43.846
but I think-
- It is scary though.

00:23:43.846 --> 00:23:44.799
- It is.

00:23:44.799 --> 00:23:46.829
I think I'll go into later with like

00:23:46.829 --> 00:23:48.979
what I think is important
when you move here

00:23:48.979 --> 00:23:49.812
and then just got to the point

00:23:49.812 --> 00:23:51.025
where I had nothing better going on

00:23:51.025 --> 00:23:51.996
and then people gave me so much shit

00:23:51.996 --> 00:23:53.525
for saying that on my video, by the way.

00:23:53.525 --> 00:23:54.358
- Oh really?

00:23:54.358 --> 00:23:56.312
- Yeah, I literally said in my video,

00:23:56.312 --> 00:23:57.939
I said, "I just had
nothing better going on,

00:23:57.939 --> 00:23:58.772
that's why I moved here."

00:23:58.772 --> 00:24:00.988
And people think that
I'm being like nonchalant

00:24:00.988 --> 00:24:02.606
or joking, like I didn't.

00:24:02.606 --> 00:24:05.285
When you're a YouTuber you
don't have anything to do

00:24:05.285 --> 00:24:07.632
other than YouTube and
hang out with your friends

00:24:07.632 --> 00:24:08.954
and do whatever you
need to do to be happy.

00:24:08.954 --> 00:24:11.180
But I literally had
nothing better going on,

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:12.852
so I'm in my head

00:24:12.852 --> 00:24:14.550
there's no reason why I wouldn't take this

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:17.495
'cause it gives me a purpose
to just better myself

00:24:17.495 --> 00:24:19.130
as a person, I get to learn new things,

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:19.963
get to meet new people.

00:24:19.963 --> 00:24:21.948
- I mean, that's a huge
reason why I moved as well as

00:24:21.948 --> 00:24:24.408
is because like in Australia like-

00:24:24.408 --> 00:24:25.642
- What are you doing?
- Right.

00:24:25.642 --> 00:24:26.983
(guys laughing)

00:24:26.983 --> 00:24:30.730
I had like great friends
from uni and high school

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:33.203
who I would hang out with
every now and then, right?

00:24:33.203 --> 00:24:36.031
And I had places to go to

00:24:36.031 --> 00:24:38.894
but it wasn't exciting anymore for me,

00:24:38.894 --> 00:24:40.672
it was just routine.
- Yeah, that's how it is.

00:24:40.672 --> 00:24:43.461
- And I hate getting into
routine with anything, right?

00:24:43.461 --> 00:24:45.754
So I was like, yeah, I mean, you know?

00:24:45.754 --> 00:24:49.085
I haven't like, yes I've spent
half my life here technically

00:24:49.085 --> 00:24:51.205
but I haven't really like gone out

00:24:51.205 --> 00:24:52.585
to fully explore the country

00:24:52.585 --> 00:24:54.624
and even four years later,

00:24:54.624 --> 00:24:56.803
I'm nowhere near close
to exploring this place.

00:24:56.803 --> 00:24:58.933
There are so many more
places I want to go to.

00:24:58.933 --> 00:25:00.293
- I still haven't left Tokyo.

00:25:00.293 --> 00:25:02.300
It's been like eight months
and I haven't left Tokyo.

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:05.631
- That road trip that
we planned is happening.

00:25:05.631 --> 00:25:07.460
All right? Once the rona
is gone, it's happening.

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:09.822
- I've left Tokyo once.

00:25:09.822 --> 00:25:11.581
(guys laughing)

00:25:11.581 --> 00:25:14.261
The decision to move
to Japan funnily enough

00:25:14.261 --> 00:25:16.514
was probably the easiest
part of the process.

00:25:16.514 --> 00:25:18.232
- Really?
- Oh, yeah.

00:25:18.232 --> 00:25:20.984
Okay, well, first of all,
I mean, you're very lucky.

00:25:20.984 --> 00:25:21.817
- Yeah.

00:25:21.817 --> 00:25:24.510
If you're half Japanese and
have a Japanese passport,

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:26.446
it's gonna be easy to move here.

00:25:26.446 --> 00:25:27.582
- Easy move, baby.

00:25:27.582 --> 00:25:30.525
- For anyone else who's
thinking about moving to Japan,

00:25:30.525 --> 00:25:34.232
whatever visa you're
thinking about coming on,

00:25:34.232 --> 00:25:35.631
it is going to be-
- It's hell, yeah.

00:25:35.631 --> 00:25:36.785
- It's tough.

00:25:36.785 --> 00:25:39.038
- And this year or this
year from, oh, sorry,

00:25:39.038 --> 00:25:41.350
from April this year I
believe they changed the rules

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:44.567
to make visas more or easier to get

00:25:44.567 --> 00:25:46.564
because they need more
immigrants to work here

00:25:46.564 --> 00:25:48.462
because the population is getting older.

00:25:48.462 --> 00:25:50.989
And they allocated 40,000 worker permits.

00:25:50.989 --> 00:25:54.129
And in the article, they only
managed to allocate 4,000

00:25:54.129 --> 00:25:55.871
this year of the 40,000.

00:25:55.871 --> 00:25:57.244
- What?
- Yeah.

00:25:57.244 --> 00:25:59.891
- Wait, it's 40,000 per year?

00:25:59.891 --> 00:26:02.733
- For some specific kind of visa

00:26:02.733 --> 00:26:04.182
for this year I think or something

00:26:04.182 --> 00:26:06.835
and they only managed to do 1/10th of that

00:26:06.835 --> 00:26:08.393
which is like not a problem

00:26:08.393 --> 00:26:10.194
because I don't think there's like,

00:26:10.194 --> 00:26:12.223
there's not demand to move here.

00:26:12.223 --> 00:26:13.543
This is probably one of the countries

00:26:13.543 --> 00:26:14.792
that I think people have most interest

00:26:14.792 --> 00:26:16.522
in moving to out of a lot of places.

00:26:16.522 --> 00:26:17.355
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:26:17.355 --> 00:26:19.625
- And it's just, I think
that if anything says a lot

00:26:19.625 --> 00:26:21.908
about how difficult it is to move here.

00:26:21.908 --> 00:26:22.741
- Yeah, exactly.
- Oh, yeah.

00:26:22.741 --> 00:26:24.911
- I mean, I can't speak on
any other visa processes,

00:26:24.911 --> 00:26:25.990
but you know?

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:28.881
- Everyone I've talked to
has had a difficult process

00:26:28.881 --> 00:26:30.823
unless you're just coming
here for a tourist visa.

00:26:30.823 --> 00:26:32.180
- Super easy.
- Super easy.

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:34.762
- Just walk in, literally just turn up.

00:26:34.762 --> 00:26:35.735
- Or if you're one of those countries

00:26:35.735 --> 00:26:37.389
that has working holiday visa.
- Yeah.

00:26:37.389 --> 00:26:40.249
- Which unfortunately America does not.

00:26:40.249 --> 00:26:41.786
So sorry Americans.

00:26:41.786 --> 00:26:43.743
- Yeah, if you want advice
on how to move here easily

00:26:43.743 --> 00:26:45.203
get a working holiday visa.

00:26:45.203 --> 00:26:46.377
It's probably gonna be-

00:26:46.377 --> 00:26:48.934
- I think those last a year and a half?

00:26:48.934 --> 00:26:50.242
- Is it a year and a half?

00:26:50.242 --> 00:26:51.656
- I think it depends on the country.

00:26:51.656 --> 00:26:53.566
At least Australians
have a year and a half.

00:26:53.566 --> 00:26:55.112
- Wow.
- I don't know about UK,

00:26:55.112 --> 00:26:59.206
maybe UK has like six months
to a year maybe, depends.

00:26:59.206 --> 00:27:00.663
- Yeah.

00:27:00.663 --> 00:27:02.566
- A lot of paperwork we had to fill out.

00:27:02.566 --> 00:27:06.576
- Yeah, I think the
hardest part of the process

00:27:06.576 --> 00:27:08.067
was finding an apartment.

00:27:08.067 --> 00:27:10.012
- Oh my God.
- Oh my God.

00:27:10.012 --> 00:27:11.452
- I mean, I'm a Japanese citizen

00:27:11.452 --> 00:27:13.578
and it was still a pain in
the ass for me to do it.

00:27:13.578 --> 00:27:16.259
- You hear about on YouTube
and Twitter and everything

00:27:16.259 --> 00:27:17.531
like it's hard, it's hard.

00:27:17.531 --> 00:27:18.615
And then you see some people saying like,

00:27:18.615 --> 00:27:20.051
"Yeah, people are over exaggerating."

00:27:20.051 --> 00:27:21.482
It is 100% hard.
- No, it's hard.

00:27:21.482 --> 00:27:23.192
- It is fucking difficult.

00:27:23.192 --> 00:27:25.508
- Especially if you're
a foreigner as well.

00:27:25.508 --> 00:27:27.458
You basically play under different rules.

00:27:27.458 --> 00:27:29.430
It's sad to say like you know,

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:31.049
and you kind of think if you have money,

00:27:31.049 --> 00:27:31.882
you get an apartment.

00:27:31.882 --> 00:27:34.311
- I think more importantly,
especially if you're a YouTuber-

00:27:34.311 --> 00:27:35.538
- Oh my God.
- Oh God.

00:27:35.538 --> 00:27:38.679
- Because the hardest
thing, at least in Japan,

00:27:38.679 --> 00:27:41.198
from my experience about why it's so hard

00:27:41.198 --> 00:27:44.167
to get a fucking apartment here is

00:27:44.167 --> 00:27:47.767
because freelancing is not a job here.

00:27:47.767 --> 00:27:49.102
- Yeah.
- Freelancing,

00:27:49.102 --> 00:27:50.685
you might as well be unemployed

00:27:50.685 --> 00:27:52.552
according to this government's-

00:27:52.552 --> 00:27:54.935
- The government's like
that typical Asian parent.

00:27:54.935 --> 00:27:56.043
- Yeah.
- Who's like, "What?

00:27:56.043 --> 00:27:58.850
You're not a doctor or
an engineer or a lawyer?"

00:27:58.850 --> 00:27:59.872
Get out of here.

00:27:59.872 --> 00:28:01.429
- You're unemployed, right?

00:28:01.429 --> 00:28:03.123
You're working part time.

00:28:03.123 --> 00:28:04.615
- And that's due to,
because they're worried

00:28:04.615 --> 00:28:06.568
about if you can pay all whatnot.

00:28:06.568 --> 00:28:07.401
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:28:07.401 --> 00:28:09.613
But the thing is, is that
they don't even like,

00:28:09.613 --> 00:28:11.483
you know, at least in like,
I don't know about the UK

00:28:11.483 --> 00:28:13.693
or Thailand, but at least in Australia,

00:28:13.693 --> 00:28:16.343
when you sign up for
like an apartment, right?

00:28:16.343 --> 00:28:18.154
Usually the landlord will be like,

00:28:18.154 --> 00:28:19.572
"Okay, can I see your bank account?"

00:28:19.572 --> 00:28:20.405
- [Garnt] Yeah.

00:28:20.405 --> 00:28:21.477
- Like just to see how much money

00:28:21.477 --> 00:28:22.560
you have saved up, you know?

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:24.597
Regardless of what job you
do, we just want to see

00:28:24.597 --> 00:28:27.738
that you have money in
the bank and if you do,

00:28:27.738 --> 00:28:28.986
then you're like okay.

00:28:28.986 --> 00:28:31.465
But here in Japan, they
don't check your bank.

00:28:31.465 --> 00:28:34.555
They check your work status to make sure

00:28:34.555 --> 00:28:37.464
that there's like a constant
flow of money coming in.

00:28:37.464 --> 00:28:38.842
- And it's not just that as well,

00:28:38.842 --> 00:28:40.577
'cause if you're not Japanese

00:28:40.577 --> 00:28:42.969
and especially if you
can't speak Japanese,

00:28:42.969 --> 00:28:45.712
most places aren't even gonna
talk to you or consider you.

00:28:45.712 --> 00:28:47.308
'Cause that was a big problem we had

00:28:47.308 --> 00:28:49.691
because we went through an agency

00:28:49.691 --> 00:28:53.949
and we just made a list of
about 10 to 20 apartments

00:28:53.949 --> 00:28:56.492
that we were interested in.

00:28:56.492 --> 00:28:58.347
And we sent it off and applied-

00:28:58.347 --> 00:29:02.378
- Rejected from all of them.
- Rejected from all of them.

00:29:02.378 --> 00:29:03.537
- Jesus Christ.

00:29:03.537 --> 00:29:05.632
- And we had a company as a guarantor

00:29:05.632 --> 00:29:08.784
so having the payments
secured wasn't the issue.

00:29:08.784 --> 00:29:11.381
It was like a lot of the time
it was really dumb stuff.

00:29:11.381 --> 00:29:14.329
Like can you sort the bins
properly and stuff like that.

00:29:14.329 --> 00:29:16.131
- It's like yes, we're not 12 year olds.

00:29:16.131 --> 00:29:18.610
- It's like the main concern
you'll hear all the time

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:20.128
is can you sort your trash properly?

00:29:20.128 --> 00:29:21.375
And it's like, okay, it might be different

00:29:21.375 --> 00:29:22.601
from where I'm from but like-

00:29:22.601 --> 00:29:23.901
- I can learn.
- Yeah.

00:29:23.901 --> 00:29:27.942
It's like the government
councils in the area

00:29:27.942 --> 00:29:30.072
give you an English manual
that tells you how to do it.

00:29:30.072 --> 00:29:32.089
It is tedious and it is a lot more complex

00:29:32.089 --> 00:29:34.340
than most places, but it's not like-

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:35.251
- It's not rocket science.

00:29:35.251 --> 00:29:37.171
- Yeah, it's not like you're having to do

00:29:37.171 --> 00:29:39.182
the weirdest stuff
imaginable to this stuff.

00:29:39.182 --> 00:29:41.191
Like it's basically just rinse your stuff,

00:29:41.191 --> 00:29:43.393
separate all of it, that's pretty much it.

00:29:43.393 --> 00:29:46.039
And I know there's some places in Europe

00:29:46.039 --> 00:29:47.255
that do it way more thorough

00:29:47.255 --> 00:29:48.596
that probably don't have this concern

00:29:48.596 --> 00:29:51.565
when they're letting people rent houses.

00:29:51.565 --> 00:29:52.398
It's just like-

00:29:52.398 --> 00:29:53.886
- Because it's the least of your concerns.

00:29:53.886 --> 00:29:54.893
It's, you know?

00:29:54.893 --> 00:29:56.315
Yeah. It's ridiculous.

00:29:56.315 --> 00:30:00.250
- Yeah and the thing is that
if you go through an agency,

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:02.508
for example, that
specializes in foreigners

00:30:02.508 --> 00:30:04.259
and people who can't speak Japanese,

00:30:04.259 --> 00:30:06.039
you're gonna be paying
so much more as well.

00:30:06.039 --> 00:30:07.341
- Yeah, you pay a premium for that bill.

00:30:07.341 --> 00:30:09.816
- You pay like a premium
for that bill and that rent.

00:30:09.816 --> 00:30:14.014
So you either have a
fucking difficult time

00:30:14.014 --> 00:30:17.926
finding a place and getting
past all the barriers

00:30:17.926 --> 00:30:20.524
or you're just gonna
pay a shit ton of money

00:30:20.524 --> 00:30:22.349
and those aren't two very good options.

00:30:22.349 --> 00:30:23.268
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:30:23.268 --> 00:30:25.483
The only concern that they often cite,

00:30:25.483 --> 00:30:27.486
building managers, etc,

00:30:27.486 --> 00:30:30.266
that I find is like somewhat,
all right, that's fair enough,

00:30:30.266 --> 00:30:31.832
is their worried that you'll run away,

00:30:31.832 --> 00:30:33.519
run away and not pay the rent.

00:30:33.519 --> 00:30:36.083
But if you have a guarantor,
that's the whole fucking point

00:30:36.083 --> 00:30:37.153
of that problem, which-

00:30:37.153 --> 00:30:38.714
- You can't run away.

00:30:38.714 --> 00:30:41.503
- If you run away, you're
still getting your money

00:30:41.503 --> 00:30:42.905
and the house is vacant.

00:30:42.905 --> 00:30:44.533
Great, no damage to it.

00:30:44.533 --> 00:30:46.339
This is a win-win for the company.

00:30:46.339 --> 00:30:47.331
I don't understand.

00:30:47.331 --> 00:30:49.492
You play by their rules in every way.

00:30:49.492 --> 00:30:51.391
- It's because it's just like typical,

00:30:51.391 --> 00:30:55.948
this is like one aspect
of Japanese work culture

00:30:55.948 --> 00:30:58.474
that I learned very quickly is that

00:30:58.474 --> 00:31:00.152
it doesn't matter what the business is.

00:31:00.152 --> 00:31:03.554
If they're not 100000000% sure

00:31:03.554 --> 00:31:05.069
that this is going to go through,

00:31:05.069 --> 00:31:06.757
it might as well be 0%.

00:31:06.757 --> 00:31:07.618
- No risk, yeah, they don't risk here.

00:31:07.618 --> 00:31:09.406
- There's no risk in any business venture,

00:31:09.406 --> 00:31:12.714
whether that be paying
for rent or anything,

00:31:12.714 --> 00:31:15.530
it's like if they not 100000000% sure

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:17.874
in every possible avenue ever,

00:31:17.874 --> 00:31:19.306
it's just not happening.

00:31:19.306 --> 00:31:22.197
And you know, it's ridiculous.

00:31:22.197 --> 00:31:24.180
- I think that'd be interesting
to talk about later on

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:25.777
when we get to more of the stuff

00:31:25.777 --> 00:31:28.425
that we've experienced here,

00:31:28.425 --> 00:31:31.723
but was there any part of the
visa process applying for it,

00:31:31.723 --> 00:31:33.682
'cause I'm sure people are
wondering, I got a lot of emails.

00:31:33.682 --> 00:31:35.104
I'm sure you did as well.

00:31:35.104 --> 00:31:37.031
People asking, "Hey,
how did you get a visa?

00:31:37.031 --> 00:31:38.050
Was it hard?"

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:39.428
Et cetera, et cetera.

00:31:39.428 --> 00:31:41.248
If there's any advice you can give someone

00:31:41.248 --> 00:31:42.961
who's watching who wants to come to Japan,

00:31:42.961 --> 00:31:44.552
what would you say is like
the main piece of advice?

00:31:44.552 --> 00:31:46.072
If they want to get a working visa?

00:31:46.072 --> 00:31:48.324
- I mean, I didn't
apply for a working visa

00:31:48.324 --> 00:31:50.837
so I don't have too
much experience with it.

00:31:50.837 --> 00:31:51.849
- We didn't apply for it.

00:31:51.849 --> 00:31:53.532
We had an immigration lawyers help us.

00:31:53.532 --> 00:31:55.102
- Yeah. We had immigration
lawyers help us,

00:31:55.102 --> 00:31:57.821
but I mean, I've applied for visas before.

00:31:57.821 --> 00:32:02.821
And the worst, the most
painful part is just waiting.

00:32:02.841 --> 00:32:04.035
- And you're gonna be waiting a lot.

00:32:04.035 --> 00:32:05.145
- You're gonna be waiting a long time

00:32:05.145 --> 00:32:08.652
and you're just gonna be
in this limbo kind of-

00:32:08.652 --> 00:32:10.150
- 'Cause we had what?

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:12.364
It was like the initial
thing where they file

00:32:12.364 --> 00:32:14.434
for something called a
Certificate of Eligibility

00:32:14.434 --> 00:32:16.452
which is basically if
the government decides

00:32:16.452 --> 00:32:19.209
if you can have a visa,
which isn't the visa itself,

00:32:19.209 --> 00:32:20.606
which already sounds to me like,

00:32:20.606 --> 00:32:22.437
that sounds like we're adding steps

00:32:22.437 --> 00:32:24.027
where steps don't need to be.

00:32:24.027 --> 00:32:24.999
But as you move to Japan,

00:32:24.999 --> 00:32:27.687
you'll learn that's what they love to do.

00:32:27.687 --> 00:32:29.040
So yeah, we had to wait, what was it?

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:30.804
Six weeks for that, six to eight weeks?

00:32:30.804 --> 00:32:31.976
- Yeah, six to eight weeks.

00:32:31.976 --> 00:32:32.890
- It could have been six to eight weeks

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:34.118
for this piece of paper to arrive,

00:32:34.118 --> 00:32:35.302
which then they send by post

00:32:35.302 --> 00:32:37.093
which makes you feel so uneasy

00:32:37.093 --> 00:32:38.652
because you're like they're
sending me a piece of paper

00:32:38.652 --> 00:32:42.123
through the mail that
I've waited two months for

00:32:42.123 --> 00:32:43.831
and you're just like
something's gonna go wrong.

00:32:43.831 --> 00:32:44.879
Someone's gonna take it.

00:32:44.879 --> 00:32:47.480
So you get this piece of paper, right?

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:49.009
To get this thing you need to prove

00:32:49.009 --> 00:32:50.750
that you have a good
reason to work in Japan,

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:52.485
that the job or, you know,

00:32:52.485 --> 00:32:54.411
'cause you have to say what your work is.

00:32:54.411 --> 00:32:55.432
They need to check that

00:32:55.432 --> 00:32:58.371
you are actually in that
field, et cetera, et cetera.

00:32:58.371 --> 00:33:01.722
- We had to send so much evidence

00:33:01.722 --> 00:33:05.717
that we were doing
valuable work that could-

00:33:05.717 --> 00:33:07.501
- Which I'm sure some people would argue

00:33:07.501 --> 00:33:09.461
YouTube isn't, but you know?

00:33:09.461 --> 00:33:10.861
At the end of the day,
if we bring in money

00:33:10.861 --> 00:33:13.435
to the government and pay taxes,
it's valuable work, right?

00:33:13.435 --> 00:33:17.645
- Yeah, basically just how many
companies we've worked with,

00:33:17.645 --> 00:33:21.019
what's work that's notable that-

00:33:21.019 --> 00:33:22.635
- And then why we need to be in Japan

00:33:22.635 --> 00:33:23.468
to do more work.

00:33:23.468 --> 00:33:25.859
- What it felt like was
I was applying for a job.

00:33:25.859 --> 00:33:29.197
It was like here's all my achievements.

00:33:29.197 --> 00:33:32.233
Here's what I can bring for your country.

00:33:32.233 --> 00:33:33.195
Please, please, please-

00:33:33.195 --> 00:33:34.294
- Let me play on your team, coach.

00:33:34.294 --> 00:33:36.334
(guys laughing)

00:33:36.334 --> 00:33:39.005
And then yeah, so we get that
Certificate of Eligibility,

00:33:39.005 --> 00:33:40.366
which is we get that.

00:33:40.366 --> 00:33:43.695
And then you have to go to the embassy.

00:33:43.695 --> 00:33:45.003
- So you turn that into a visa

00:33:45.003 --> 00:33:46.096
and then they'll put a-

00:33:46.096 --> 00:33:48.651
- Yeah, which again, there's more steps

00:33:48.651 --> 00:33:51.520
'cause first of all,
if you live in the UK,

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:52.885
the only embassy is in London.

00:33:52.885 --> 00:33:54.291
Now I would feel bad for someone

00:33:54.291 --> 00:33:56.341
who lives in the North of Scotland.

00:33:56.341 --> 00:33:58.410
I don't think they have
an Embassy in Scotland.

00:33:58.410 --> 00:33:59.243
- No, no.

00:33:59.243 --> 00:34:01.023
- In Wales, I had to go all
the way from Wales that,

00:34:01.023 --> 00:34:03.217
I mean, luckily there's
fast train, doesn't matter.

00:34:03.217 --> 00:34:04.507
But point is one embassy,

00:34:04.507 --> 00:34:06.502
so you're going to be taking
a day trip to get this thing.

00:34:06.502 --> 00:34:09.515
Worst part is, is that then
if you go to the embassy,

00:34:09.515 --> 00:34:12.326
which you might've have to
traveled to across country for,

00:34:12.326 --> 00:34:15.449
you have to wait five days
to then actually get the visa

00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:16.836
which is a little thing
they just print out

00:34:16.836 --> 00:34:18.356
and staple in your passport.

00:34:18.356 --> 00:34:19.362
- So you have to go back to the embassy.

00:34:19.362 --> 00:34:20.889
- Yeah. You have to go back, right?

00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:21.990
So I just went home,

00:34:21.990 --> 00:34:24.309
'cause I'm like not staying
in London for five days.

00:34:24.309 --> 00:34:27.580
If you're poor, you haven't got much money

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:28.700
and you can't, you know,

00:34:28.700 --> 00:34:30.655
the train tickets in the
UK are expensive, right?

00:34:30.655 --> 00:34:31.488
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:34:31.488 --> 00:34:33.049
- There's a lot of things
where it's just like, my God,

00:34:33.049 --> 00:34:34.149
why are we doing this?

00:34:34.149 --> 00:34:35.423
- But then again like realistically,

00:34:35.423 --> 00:34:38.413
how many people in the butt
fuck middle of Scotland

00:34:38.413 --> 00:34:39.669
is going to apply for a visa-

00:34:39.669 --> 00:34:42.040
- I'm sure, I bet there's one person from

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:44.229
like the North Island in like Scotland,

00:34:44.229 --> 00:34:46.539
who's like, "Dude, I had
a fly, fuck that shit."

00:34:46.539 --> 00:34:49.149
- If you are that one
person watching, subscribe.

00:34:49.149 --> 00:34:50.891
(guys laughing)

00:34:50.891 --> 00:34:54.206
- And to make matters even more stressful,

00:34:54.206 --> 00:34:55.702
you lost your passport.
- I did.

00:34:55.702 --> 00:34:56.785
- You lost your passport.

00:34:56.785 --> 00:34:58.988
- This mother, we were
meeting up at the embassy

00:34:58.988 --> 00:35:00.669
to collect our visas

00:35:00.669 --> 00:35:03.338
and this mother fucker lost his passport.

00:35:03.338 --> 00:35:05.932
- When I heard that story,
I couldn't fucking believe.

00:35:05.932 --> 00:35:06.765
- Oh my God.

00:35:06.765 --> 00:35:08.065
That was like one of the
worst days of my life.

00:35:08.065 --> 00:35:09.668
- Like you can't write that shit.

00:35:09.668 --> 00:35:11.048
- I know, it was horrible.

00:35:11.048 --> 00:35:13.988
- All because you didn't
have a fucking backpack.

00:35:13.988 --> 00:35:16.638
- I carry my passport in my jeans pocket,

00:35:16.638 --> 00:35:18.275
which I'm hearing people like oh my God.

00:35:18.275 --> 00:35:19.647
- The worst!
- Oh my God!

00:35:19.647 --> 00:35:20.850
- The worst idea.

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:23.016
- I'm like cringing hearing that, man.

00:35:23.016 --> 00:35:23.849
I'm like.

00:35:23.849 --> 00:35:26.198
- So okay, I've traveled
like all over the world

00:35:26.198 --> 00:35:27.912
just having my passport in my pocket.

00:35:27.912 --> 00:35:29.929
Okay, I have lost it before
and you guys know I have.

00:35:29.929 --> 00:35:30.942
I lost it in America as well.

00:35:30.942 --> 00:35:33.055
I found it though luckily, but anyway,

00:35:33.055 --> 00:35:36.523
so I was going to the train
station to go to the train,

00:35:36.523 --> 00:35:37.591
go to London.

00:35:37.591 --> 00:35:40.657
And as my train arrived, I
always tap my pockets, right,

00:35:40.657 --> 00:35:41.757
just to make sure it's there.

00:35:41.757 --> 00:35:43.519
I tap my pockets.

00:35:43.519 --> 00:35:46.248
It's not in there, it's not I'm there.

00:35:46.248 --> 00:35:47.634
And I'm like fuck.

00:35:47.634 --> 00:35:49.420
And what's running through my head is

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:51.733
all of this months of work,

00:35:51.733 --> 00:35:53.901
I feel bad for the company,
they paid for lawyers.

00:35:53.901 --> 00:35:56.713
All these people who
have banked on me coming

00:35:56.713 --> 00:35:58.861
and I've put so much work into this,

00:35:58.861 --> 00:35:59.959
myself and other people.

00:35:59.959 --> 00:36:01.898
And I was like having a mental breakdown.

00:36:01.898 --> 00:36:03.647
I don't think I've ever had that much

00:36:03.647 --> 00:36:05.836
of a quick mental breakdown in my life.

00:36:05.836 --> 00:36:06.669
- Yeah.

00:36:08.284 --> 00:36:09.152
It's to be expected.

00:36:09.152 --> 00:36:10.902
- And it was like gradually getting worse

00:36:10.902 --> 00:36:13.407
in the hour I couldn't find it.

00:36:13.407 --> 00:36:16.851
I scoured this train station,
a tiny train station in Wales.

00:36:16.851 --> 00:36:18.100
It's called Flint.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:18.933
You can look it up.

00:36:18.933 --> 00:36:21.169
It's literally just like a platform.

00:36:21.169 --> 00:36:22.485
There's no where this should have gone

00:36:22.485 --> 00:36:23.391
where I couldn't have found it, right?

00:36:23.391 --> 00:36:24.410
Could not find it.

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:27.689
Check my car, quickly drove
home to find it, didn't find it.

00:36:27.689 --> 00:36:29.584
Came back to check
again, couldn't find it.

00:36:29.584 --> 00:36:30.884
Go in to the police station.

00:36:30.884 --> 00:36:32.068
Literally everything.

00:36:32.068 --> 00:36:32.901
I could not find my passport.

00:36:32.901 --> 00:36:33.963
- So you have no idea what happened to it?

00:36:33.963 --> 00:36:35.699
- I have no idea what
happened to my passport.

00:36:35.699 --> 00:36:38.595
And then eventually it was
getting like three, 4:00 pm

00:36:38.595 --> 00:36:40.906
and I was like, okay,
I'm gonna just call up

00:36:40.906 --> 00:36:43.128
the British government and tell them,

00:36:43.128 --> 00:36:44.378
hey, can you just cancel my passport.

00:36:44.378 --> 00:36:45.629
I wanna apply for another one.

00:36:45.629 --> 00:36:46.929
And that whole thing was an ordeal.

00:36:46.929 --> 00:36:48.587
And then my main concern
was like, all right,

00:36:48.587 --> 00:36:49.890
well, I lost my passport.

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:52.691
Do I have to restart the
whole visa process again?

00:36:52.691 --> 00:36:53.680
Luckily the embassy were like,

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:55.791
"No, as long as you have the certificate",

00:36:55.791 --> 00:36:57.048
which I didn't lose.

00:36:57.048 --> 00:36:58.867
Great. I could redo it.

00:36:58.867 --> 00:36:59.847
- You lucky fucker.

00:36:59.847 --> 00:37:02.340
- I had to scramble to get a new passport

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:03.487
and that was a whole thing.

00:37:03.487 --> 00:37:05.786
And I got one within
like five days luckily.

00:37:05.786 --> 00:37:09.045
And luckily it all went well,
but it was just so stressful

00:37:09.045 --> 00:37:11.283
on top of already the stress that,

00:37:11.283 --> 00:37:12.951
I was already at my limit with stress

00:37:12.951 --> 00:37:15.351
that whole period, because
of this whole thing

00:37:15.351 --> 00:37:16.711
and that was just like the full,

00:37:16.711 --> 00:37:18.173
I had a full breakdown that that day.

00:37:18.173 --> 00:37:19.015
That was horrible.

00:37:19.015 --> 00:37:20.465
And I think I like joked
about it on Twitter,

00:37:20.465 --> 00:37:22.181
but like I was like not okay that day.

00:37:22.181 --> 00:37:23.318
- Oh yeah.
- No, no, no.

00:37:23.318 --> 00:37:24.383
- And the worst part is, right,

00:37:24.383 --> 00:37:26.093
is when you fuck up this badly,

00:37:26.093 --> 00:37:27.655
you can accept that yo fucked up

00:37:27.655 --> 00:37:29.907
but it's messaging your
friends being like,

00:37:29.907 --> 00:37:32.187
"Hey, I'm not gonna be
in London in two hours."

00:37:32.187 --> 00:37:33.665
They're like, "Oh, why, what's up?"

00:37:33.665 --> 00:37:34.671
I'm like, "I lost my passport."

00:37:34.671 --> 00:37:36.342
(guys laughing)

00:37:36.342 --> 00:37:38.275
- I remember reading that text,

00:37:38.275 --> 00:37:42.048
'cause I was waiting for
you outside the embassy

00:37:42.048 --> 00:37:45.187
when you were like, I get a
message from Connor being like,

00:37:45.187 --> 00:37:47.357
"I'm gonna be a bit like 30 minutes late."

00:37:47.357 --> 00:37:48.955
(guys laughing)

00:37:48.955 --> 00:37:49.788
- Little did I know.

00:37:49.788 --> 00:37:51.977
- Was like oh, all
right, I'll get a coffee.

00:37:51.977 --> 00:37:55.988
And I see the event unfold
as he's just texting me.

00:37:55.988 --> 00:37:59.040
So he goes, "Yeah, I
can't find my passport.

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:02.776
I'm just gonna go look for it quickly."

00:38:02.776 --> 00:38:07.310
And then it was just you
like just breaking down

00:38:07.310 --> 00:38:10.440
every message and it just ends with,

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:12.522
"Yeah, I don't think I can come."

00:38:12.522 --> 00:38:13.872
- And I'm still annoyed to this day

00:38:13.872 --> 00:38:14.705
because I went around,

00:38:14.705 --> 00:38:16.350
this is a very small town where I lost it.

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.210
I went around like
every shop in that area.

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:20.017
I asked literally everyone in that area.

00:38:20.017 --> 00:38:23.018
Hey, if you find this passport,
can you call me right away?

00:38:23.018 --> 00:38:25.500
Because if not, I'm gonna
just cancel it today.

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:26.971
I still never heard anything.

00:38:26.971 --> 00:38:29.610
I was crazy and I don't
know how I lost it.

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:32.968
- So from your experience
for anyone who's listening

00:38:32.968 --> 00:38:34.534
if there's anything they need to know

00:38:34.534 --> 00:38:36.733
to make the process easier,
what would you guys say?

00:38:36.733 --> 00:38:37.946
- Get a degree.

00:38:37.946 --> 00:38:39.808
- Yeah, a degree helps a
lot, especially if it's-

00:38:39.808 --> 00:38:43.492
- Not an associate's,
like a bachelor's degree.

00:38:43.492 --> 00:38:45.017
- Yeah, I think a lot of their forms

00:38:45.017 --> 00:38:46.569
didn't recognize associate's.

00:38:46.569 --> 00:38:48.835
I think it actually said, it's
specified like bachelor's.

00:38:48.835 --> 00:38:51.454
- I don't think associates
is a thing in Japan.

00:38:51.454 --> 00:38:52.711
- It's not a thing in
the UK, I don't think.

00:38:52.711 --> 00:38:53.555
- Yeah, it's not a thing.

00:38:53.555 --> 00:38:54.670
- It's not a thing in Japan, right?

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:56.019
I mean, it is a thing,

00:38:56.019 --> 00:38:59.008
but like it's not as heavily emphasized

00:38:59.008 --> 00:39:01.175
on as a bachelor's degree.

00:39:02.429 --> 00:39:04.670
- I saw a lot of people are asking like,

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:06.293
Oh, how do we get it, et cetera.

00:39:06.293 --> 00:39:09.483
You need to have, especially
with a country that's strict,

00:39:09.483 --> 00:39:11.578
you need to have concrete proof

00:39:11.578 --> 00:39:13.944
that you can do something here.

00:39:13.944 --> 00:39:15.571
You can't just come here on a whim.

00:39:15.571 --> 00:39:17.192
- That you're worth something.

00:39:17.192 --> 00:39:19.669
- Unless you are like
extremely, extremely rich

00:39:19.669 --> 00:39:22.360
and they want your tax money
I think the only other reason,

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:23.802
like you need some good reason.

00:39:23.802 --> 00:39:25.962
- Even then you need a
degree for something anyway.

00:39:25.962 --> 00:39:26.920
- They'll find a way to-

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:28.025
- Yeah, 'cause then they'll be like,

00:39:28.025 --> 00:39:29.659
"Where are you getting
this money from, bro?"

00:39:29.659 --> 00:39:32.668
- And I imagine just having
like a fair amount of experience

00:39:32.668 --> 00:39:33.620
in what you're doing

00:39:33.620 --> 00:39:35.316
and the reason for coming
here will help a ton.

00:39:35.316 --> 00:39:36.372
I mean, I can't talk about most,

00:39:36.372 --> 00:39:37.567
just what we went through.

00:39:37.567 --> 00:39:39.374
- Or basically just come
here to teach English.

00:39:39.374 --> 00:39:41.788
- That is like the instant get in button.

00:39:41.788 --> 00:39:43.035
- Yeah. Pretty much.

00:39:43.035 --> 00:39:45.444
It's like I can speak English
and I can teach it somewhat

00:39:45.444 --> 00:39:46.842
then they're like you're hired.

00:39:46.842 --> 00:39:48.927
- It's probably not a bad way to,

00:39:48.927 --> 00:39:50.889
if your goal ultimately is
living in Japan forever,

00:39:50.889 --> 00:39:53.199
it's probably not a bad way to
transition into living here.

00:39:53.199 --> 00:39:55.370
- No, because I know a
lot of friends personally

00:39:55.370 --> 00:39:57.569
who did start off with teaching English

00:39:57.569 --> 00:39:59.656
and then gradually moved
on to something else.

00:39:59.656 --> 00:40:01.667
- 'Cause by teaching English,

00:40:01.667 --> 00:40:03.995
you can gain connections in the country

00:40:03.995 --> 00:40:06.044
and then maybe that transitions

00:40:06.044 --> 00:40:08.543
to an like a normal office
job or something like that

00:40:08.543 --> 00:40:09.840
or working with a company.

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:11.985
But if you just want to get
your foot through the door,

00:40:11.985 --> 00:40:14.295
I think teaching English
would be the easiest way

00:40:14.295 --> 00:40:17.085
for anyone to be able to do it.

00:40:17.085 --> 00:40:18.269
But you still need a degree.

00:40:18.269 --> 00:40:20.023
- Yeah, I agree, you need a degree.

00:40:20.023 --> 00:40:21.903
- But they pretty much
accept everyone on that,

00:40:21.903 --> 00:40:24.581
as far as I'm aware as long as,
again, if you have a degree.

00:40:24.581 --> 00:40:25.637
- Yeah, It doesn't matter how good

00:40:25.637 --> 00:40:26.996
your high school essays were.

00:40:26.996 --> 00:40:29.776
If you don't have a degree in that, yeah.

00:40:29.776 --> 00:40:32.187
- Which is why I'm so glad
to drop out of university.

00:40:32.187 --> 00:40:34.083
- Listen to your Asian parents, go to uni

00:40:34.083 --> 00:40:35.125
if you want to go to Japan.

00:40:35.125 --> 00:40:36.290
- You guys have all got degrees, right?

00:40:36.290 --> 00:40:37.123
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:40:37.123 --> 00:40:37.956
- Yeah, yeah, okay.

00:40:37.956 --> 00:40:39.144
- Engineering baby, let's go.

00:40:39.144 --> 00:40:40.220
- Oh really?
- Yeah. Engineering.

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:41.843
- I never actually have known what degree,

00:40:41.843 --> 00:40:43.097
you have an engineering degree, right?

00:40:43.097 --> 00:40:43.930
- I have.

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:44.763
- Yeah, he has an electrical engineering

00:40:44.763 --> 00:40:45.906
and I have mechanical engineering.

00:40:45.906 --> 00:40:47.011
- Oh, wow.
- But he has a master's,

00:40:47.011 --> 00:40:47.844
I have a bachelor's.

00:40:47.844 --> 00:40:49.357
- I got a fucking master's mother fucker.

00:40:49.357 --> 00:40:50.737
(guys laughing)

00:40:50.737 --> 00:40:54.567
- Look at this boy spending
more than four years at uni.

00:40:54.567 --> 00:40:55.439
- I got to the third year,

00:40:55.439 --> 00:40:56.568
I was like, I can't do it anymore.

00:40:56.568 --> 00:40:57.479
I can't do this.

00:40:57.479 --> 00:40:58.312
I gotta get out, I gotta get out.

00:40:58.312 --> 00:41:00.031
- Connor's like have I
got the required minimum?

00:41:00.031 --> 00:41:01.224
Yeah. Get me out of here.

00:41:01.224 --> 00:41:02.262
(guys laughing)

00:41:02.262 --> 00:41:04.226
I don't want to be here
for another year, dude.

00:41:04.226 --> 00:41:06.350
- So now that you guys moved to Japan,

00:41:06.350 --> 00:41:08.890
what would you say are the pros and cons

00:41:08.890 --> 00:41:10.080
of actually living here?

00:41:10.080 --> 00:41:12.366
- I mean, I want to know
from you guys, right?

00:41:12.366 --> 00:41:14.845
Because I mean, I've experienced Japan

00:41:14.845 --> 00:41:17.008
pretty much for half my life so I know,

00:41:17.008 --> 00:41:20.604
to me the pros and cons are
just very natural to me.

00:41:20.604 --> 00:41:22.540
- And I feel like you,
the most of the cons

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:25.427
that we might, me and Garnt might have,

00:41:25.427 --> 00:41:27.359
you won't ever experience because one,

00:41:27.359 --> 00:41:29.231
you can speak the language
and read it fluently.

00:41:29.231 --> 00:41:30.119
- [Garnt] Yeah.

00:41:30.119 --> 00:41:32.608
- I don't know, man, I have
the face of a foreigner.

00:41:32.608 --> 00:41:33.624
- Yeah, true.

00:41:33.624 --> 00:41:35.903
- Like I'm just gonna say it.

00:41:35.903 --> 00:41:37.317
There's a lot of discrimination here.

00:41:37.317 --> 00:41:39.716
(guys laughing)

00:41:39.716 --> 00:41:41.513
- But I mean, what country doesn't?

00:41:41.513 --> 00:41:44.854
- I would say, I like understand it more

00:41:44.854 --> 00:41:48.104
because growing up in an Asian country,

00:41:49.323 --> 00:41:51.463
there's definitely discrimination in Asia.

00:41:51.463 --> 00:41:54.243
It's just very different from
what you see in the West.

00:41:54.243 --> 00:41:55.111
I think.
- That's the thing,

00:41:55.111 --> 00:41:57.192
it's not racism.

00:41:57.192 --> 00:41:59.893
It's more xenophobia.
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:41:59.893 --> 00:42:01.635
- Right, and if you guys
don't know the difference

00:42:01.635 --> 00:42:04.285
between that, racism is where it's like,

00:42:04.285 --> 00:42:06.108
oh, you're a different race to me

00:42:06.108 --> 00:42:08.046
so therefore you are worse than me.

00:42:08.046 --> 00:42:10.744
And xenophobia is like,
oh, you're not from here

00:42:10.744 --> 00:42:12.659
therefore you're, you know?

00:42:12.659 --> 00:42:15.495
- I think it comes more
from a place of ignorance.

00:42:15.495 --> 00:42:16.328
- It's just ignorance I think.

00:42:16.328 --> 00:42:18.542
- 'Cause it's just, you know,

00:42:18.542 --> 00:42:21.273
'cause you guys didn't
open the country for ages.

00:42:21.273 --> 00:42:22.359
- I mean, Japan has been a closed island

00:42:22.359 --> 00:42:23.353
for like the past 2000 years, right?

00:42:23.353 --> 00:42:24.403
- Exactly.
- So it's like,

00:42:24.403 --> 00:42:26.673
what do you expect?
- Exactly.

00:42:26.673 --> 00:42:28.673
So it makes sense to me.

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:33.530
But like for example,
finding an apartment,

00:42:33.530 --> 00:42:36.101
that was my first experience with that

00:42:36.101 --> 00:42:38.471
where if you didn't,
if you're not Japanese

00:42:38.471 --> 00:42:41.319
and if you didn't speak Japanese

00:42:41.319 --> 00:42:44.567
then they were very scared
to even touch you, you know?

00:42:44.567 --> 00:42:47.013
Or even consider you in that case.

00:42:47.013 --> 00:42:48.371
- Especially when you have
a company backing you.

00:42:48.371 --> 00:42:49.462
In my mind, I was like,

00:42:49.462 --> 00:42:50.750
I don't see what the problem is here.

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:53.168
I'm like the model tenant here.

00:42:53.168 --> 00:42:54.212
I'm single.

00:42:54.212 --> 00:42:56.798
- The problem is we can't
write your name in Kanji.

00:42:56.798 --> 00:42:58.864
- Yeah, right?
- That's the problem.

00:42:58.864 --> 00:43:01.227
- Kata Connor name? Ew, gross.

00:43:01.227 --> 00:43:02.757
- But I mean, I have a problem too,

00:43:02.757 --> 00:43:05.648
because even though I am Japanese

00:43:05.648 --> 00:43:07.659
and I have a Japanese name,

00:43:07.659 --> 00:43:09.976
the fact that I don't look it,

00:43:09.976 --> 00:43:11.809
is already a huge wall

00:43:13.361 --> 00:43:16.179
that regular Japanese
people find a difficulty

00:43:16.179 --> 00:43:18.741
in getting over because,
it's really funny as well,

00:43:18.741 --> 00:43:20.813
because during my intern days as well,

00:43:20.813 --> 00:43:22.665
I had to be the guy to like

00:43:22.665 --> 00:43:24.987
if there was like a business
partner coming to the office,

00:43:24.987 --> 00:43:27.129
I had to be the one to
get on the phone with them

00:43:27.129 --> 00:43:30.175
and being like, "Okay, you
guys are coming at this time

00:43:30.175 --> 00:43:32.923
and this day, okay, I'll
come down to meet you guys

00:43:32.923 --> 00:43:34.444
and greet you up to the office."

00:43:34.444 --> 00:43:36.089
But when I speak Japanese,

00:43:36.089 --> 00:43:38.892
I don't have an accent
and my name is Japanese.

00:43:38.892 --> 00:43:41.464
So there were so many
times where I would go down

00:43:41.464 --> 00:43:44.282
to greet them and they'd
be like who the fuck-

00:43:44.282 --> 00:43:46.207
(guys laughing)

00:43:46.207 --> 00:43:48.180
She's like, "You're not the
person on the phone, right?"

00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:49.881
And I was like, "Yeah, I am."

00:43:49.881 --> 00:43:51.656
And it's like, "Oh, Oh, we
thought you were Japanese."

00:43:51.656 --> 00:43:53.490
And I'm like, "I am Japanese."

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:55.278
And they're like.

00:43:55.278 --> 00:43:59.073
To so many of like the older
generation, especially,

00:43:59.073 --> 00:44:01.461
they just can't fathom that there are

00:44:01.461 --> 00:44:03.628
these things called hafus.

00:44:04.654 --> 00:44:05.684
It's just not a thing.

00:44:05.684 --> 00:44:08.027
It's like you're either
Japanese or you're not Japanese.

00:44:08.027 --> 00:44:08.860
- Yeah.

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:11.918
'Cause the way I it is that,

00:44:11.918 --> 00:44:13.836
I treat it like exactly the same

00:44:13.836 --> 00:44:18.364
how I would treat my
grandmother or my granddad

00:44:18.364 --> 00:44:21.610
who lived in Thailand
in this small village

00:44:21.610 --> 00:44:22.585
their entire fucking life,

00:44:22.585 --> 00:44:25.354
knew nothing about the outside world.

00:44:25.354 --> 00:44:27.149
So they just didn't know.

00:44:27.149 --> 00:44:28.351
- Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:44:28.351 --> 00:44:30.708
- It's the same way with
a lot of Japanese people

00:44:30.708 --> 00:44:33.439
where some of them,
especially like older people

00:44:33.439 --> 00:44:35.936
who've never interacted with a foreigner

00:44:35.936 --> 00:44:39.196
or even like got to
know them or even knows

00:44:39.196 --> 00:44:40.835
what the outside world is like

00:44:40.835 --> 00:44:43.515
beyond what they see on the
news and what they see on TV.

00:44:43.515 --> 00:44:44.438
You know what I mean?

00:44:44.438 --> 00:44:46.827
- And you don't want
to like input anything

00:44:46.827 --> 00:44:48.107
from Japanese TV.

00:44:48.107 --> 00:44:49.820
- No, no, you don't.

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:50.653
- My god.

00:44:50.653 --> 00:44:51.680
Some of the stuff they air is like-

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:53.340
- We're called Trash Taste Podcast.

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:56.273
If there's anything trashier
than Trash Taste Podcast

00:44:56.273 --> 00:44:58.066
it is fucking Japanese TV.

00:44:58.066 --> 00:45:00.148
It is full of absolute garbage.

00:45:00.148 --> 00:45:02.955
- Yeah.
- It's something I wish that

00:45:02.955 --> 00:45:05.117
like a lot of YouTubers whose channel

00:45:05.117 --> 00:45:07.170
is focused all about Japan

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:09.042
would speak about it a little more.

00:45:09.042 --> 00:45:10.811
'Cause it's not something you can ignore.

00:45:10.811 --> 00:45:14.270
Like if you're foreign or even
look foreign and live here,

00:45:14.270 --> 00:45:16.621
you're gonna experience it like totally.

00:45:16.621 --> 00:45:19.019
And I didn't know anything about this

00:45:19.019 --> 00:45:21.243
because no YouTubers talk
about it or anything.

00:45:21.243 --> 00:45:23.913
And I feel like it's
such an important topic

00:45:23.913 --> 00:45:26.000
especially when you talk
about living in Japan,

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:29.047
it's gonna be a factor,
you're gonna be stared at.

00:45:29.047 --> 00:45:30.574
And it's not like you've said,

00:45:30.574 --> 00:45:32.826
it's not a place of
like hatred or anything.

00:45:32.826 --> 00:45:36.424
It's just like they don't
see kind of people a lot.

00:45:36.424 --> 00:45:38.729
- I think the problem
was a lot of J-Vloggers

00:45:38.729 --> 00:45:40.348
that do cover the topics like that,

00:45:40.348 --> 00:45:42.318
'cause when you live here,

00:45:42.318 --> 00:45:44.122
you pretty much get to
know every J-Vlogger.

00:45:44.122 --> 00:45:46.700
It's just natural, it's
such a small community.

00:45:46.700 --> 00:45:49.402
So I know pretty much every
like notable J-Vlogger here.

00:45:49.402 --> 00:45:50.869
And there's been a number
of them that I know

00:45:50.869 --> 00:45:53.663
that have made topics on
the blatant xenophobia

00:45:53.663 --> 00:45:54.903
here in Japan.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:54.903 --> 00:45:57.211
- But the problem is I
think a lot of those videos

00:45:57.211 --> 00:45:58.904
aren't taken seriously by the internet

00:45:58.904 --> 00:46:02.206
because it's seen as like
a privileged problem.

00:46:02.206 --> 00:46:04.221
It's like you get to
live in Japan, you know?

00:46:04.221 --> 00:46:05.926
So therefore you should just be able

00:46:05.926 --> 00:46:07.156
to put up with this shit, right?

00:46:07.156 --> 00:46:09.345
Because you get to experience
all this other cool stuff.

00:46:09.345 --> 00:46:12.337
When that's not the point
of the video, right?

00:46:12.337 --> 00:46:15.228
Like it's still a problem
regardless of how privileged

00:46:15.228 --> 00:46:17.019
you may seem living here.

00:46:17.019 --> 00:46:19.190
- I think the worst
thing that happened to me

00:46:19.190 --> 00:46:22.499
was the time, one time we
got the police called on us.

00:46:22.499 --> 00:46:23.416
- Oh, yeah.

00:46:25.204 --> 00:46:26.746
- Do you wanna explain why
we got the police called?

00:46:26.746 --> 00:46:28.204
We didn't do anything illegal.

00:46:28.204 --> 00:46:29.963
We just played Mario Party really loud.

00:46:29.963 --> 00:46:31.858
- Yeah at like two in the morning.

00:46:31.858 --> 00:46:33.080
(guys laughing)

00:46:33.080 --> 00:46:33.913
Very drunk.

00:46:33.913 --> 00:46:35.097
- That was our bad.

00:46:35.097 --> 00:46:37.379
We can't really complain about that.

00:46:37.379 --> 00:46:40.220
Okay, so basically we've
got a noise complaint.

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:44.556
So the policeman came and
he basically asked Joey,

00:46:44.556 --> 00:46:46.063
who is he with?
- Yeah.

00:46:46.063 --> 00:46:47.784
- And I remember you saying like,

00:46:47.784 --> 00:46:51.402
"Oh, there's one American,
one British person."

00:46:51.402 --> 00:46:54.329
And then there was me, who I'm Thai.

00:46:54.329 --> 00:46:56.935
So he mentioned that
I'm with a Thai friend.

00:46:56.935 --> 00:47:00.517
And as soon as he mentioned
that he was with a Thai friend,

00:47:00.517 --> 00:47:02.533
the policeman was like, "Oh."

00:47:02.533 --> 00:47:04.493
- "Can we see your Thai friend?"

00:47:04.493 --> 00:47:05.820
- "Can we see your Thai friend?"

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:09.869
And I go up there and I
started getting grilled

00:47:09.869 --> 00:47:11.711
about why I'm here.

00:47:11.711 --> 00:47:13.619
They want to see my
passport and everything.

00:47:13.619 --> 00:47:15.668
And they asked me for my visa.

00:47:15.668 --> 00:47:17.487
- But you showed them your
British passport, right?

00:47:17.487 --> 00:47:19.381
- No, as soon I was like,

00:47:19.381 --> 00:47:21.238
"Oh, I have a British passport though."

00:47:21.238 --> 00:47:23.263
They were like, "Okay, you're free to go."

00:47:23.263 --> 00:47:25.521
(guys laughing)

